  ("1.6. CHAPTER REMARKS" 0.1924 0.4283 0.1044 0.1212 0.1212 0.0168 0.0145 0.2739 0.0284)
  ("17" 0.7934 0.8117 0.1076 0.1194 0.1194 0.0117 nil nil 0.0182)
  ("In summary, qsort has sorted the example list into numerical order. More" 0.1924 0.8117 0.1441 0.1558 0.1558 0.0117 0.0082 0.2921 0.0169)
  ("generally, this function produces a sorted version of any list of numbers. The" 0.1924 0.8122 0.1602 0.1719 0.1719 0.0117 0.0063 0.2501 0.0751)
  ("ﬁrst equation for qsort states that the empty list is already sorted, while the" 0.1924 0.8118 0.1763 0.1880 0.1880 0.0117 0.0066 0.2387 0.0319)
  ("second states that any non-empty list can be sorted by inserting the ﬁrst" 0.1924 0.8121 0.1924 0.2042 0.2042 0.0117 0.0090 0.1932 0.0530)
  ("number between the two lists that result from sorting the remaining numbers" 0.1924 0.8122 0.2085 0.2203 0.2203 0.0117 0.0059 0.2983 0.0609)
  ("that are smaller and larger than this number. This method of sorting is called" 0.1924 0.8115 0.2246 0.2364 0.2364 0.0117 0.0051 0.3077 0.0336)
  ("quicksort, and is one of the best such methods known." 0.1924 0.6287 0.2407 0.2525 0.2525 0.0117 0.0062 0.2474 0.0783)
  ("The above implementation of quicksort is an excellent example of the power" 0.2208 0.8117 0.2567 0.2684 0.2684 0.0117 0.0043 0.3352 0.0317)
  ("of Haskell, being both clear and concise. Moreover, the function qsort is also" 0.1924 0.8117 0.2728 0.2845 0.2845 0.0117 0.0065 0.2621 0.0147)
  ("more general than might be expected, being applicable not just with numbers," 0.1924 0.8122 0.2889 0.3006 0.3006 0.0117 0.0052 0.3045 0.0398)
  ("but with any type of ordered values. More precisely, the type" 0.1924 0.6855 0.3050 0.3167 0.3167 0.0117 0.0062 0.2669 0.0277)
  ("qsort :: Ord a ⇒ [a ] → [a ]" 0.3919 0.6041 0.3355 0.3524 0.3505 0.0117 0.0051 0.4971 0.0390)
  ("states that for any type a of ordered values, qsort is a function that maps" 0.1924 0.8120 0.3657 0.3775 0.3774 0.0117 0.0080 0.1999 0.0457)
  ("between lists of such values. Haskell supports many diﬀerent types of ordered" 0.1924 0.8119 0.3818 0.3936 0.3936 0.0117 0.0059 0.2630 0.0645)
  ("values, including numbers, single characters such as ’a’, and strings of char-" 0.1924 0.8117 0.3979 0.4100 0.4097 0.0117 0.0064 0.2398 0.0535)
  ("acters such as \"abcde\". Hence, for example, the function qsort could also be" 0.1924 0.8121 0.4140 0.4261 0.4258 0.0117 0.0065 0.2390 0.0467)
  ("used to sort a list of characters, or a list of strings." 0.1924 0.6004 0.4301 0.4419 0.4419 0.0117 0.0062 0.2017 0.0358)
  ("1.6" 0.1924 0.2270 0.4681 0.4834 0.4834 0.0153 nil 0.2899 0.0346)
  ("Chapter remarks" 0.2544 0.4554 0.4681 0.4834 0.4834 0.0153 0.0091 0.2597 0.0968)
  ("The Haskell Report is freely available on the web from the Haskell home page," 0.1924 0.8117 0.4998 0.5116 0.5116 0.0117 0.0053 0.3199 0.0317)
  ("www .haskell .org , and has also been published as a book [26]. A more detailed" 0.1924 0.8115 0.5159 0.5277 0.5277 0.0117 0.0055 0.3313 0.0364)
  ("historical account of the development of functional programming languages is" 0.1924 0.8119 0.5320 0.5438 0.5438 0.0117 0.0060 0.2799 0.0733)
  ("given in Hudak’s survey article [11]." 0.1924 0.4805 0.5482 0.5599 0.5599 0.0117 0.0062 0.2610 0.0415)
  ("1.7" 0.1924 0.2270 0.5861 0.6014 0.6014 0.0153 nil 0.2864 0.0346)
  ("Exercises" 0.2544 0.3630 0.5861 0.6014 0.6014 0.0153 nil 0.2252 0.1086)
  ("1. Give another possible calculation for the result of double (double 2)." 0.2148 0.7834 0.6178 0.6296 0.6296 0.0117 0.0063 0.2403 0.0142)
  ("2. Show that sum [x ] = x for any number x ." 0.2148 0.5757 0.6446 0.6564 0.6564 0.0117 0.0061 0.3319 0.0142)
  ("3. Deﬁne a function product that produces the product of a list of numbers," 0.2148 0.8121 0.6714 0.6832 0.6831 0.0117 0.0050 0.2760 0.0142)
  ("and show using your deﬁnition that product [2, 3, 4] = 24." 0.2384 0.7005 0.6875 0.6993 0.6992 0.0117 0.0060 0.2866 0.0296)
  ("4. How should the deﬁnition of the function qsort be modiﬁed so that it" 0.2148 0.8119 0.7142 0.7259 0.7259 0.0117 0.0074 0.2440 0.0142)
  ("produces a reverse sorted version of a list?" 0.2384 0.5806 0.7303 0.7420 0.7420 0.0117 0.0062 0.1917 0.0712)
  ("5. What would be the eﬀect of replacing ⩽ by < in the deﬁnition of qsort?" 0.2148 0.8118 0.7538 0.7707 0.7688 0.0117 0.0057 0.3112 0.0142)
  ("Hint: consider the example qsort [2, 2, 3, 1, 1]." 0.2384 0.6041 0.7732 0.7849 0.7849 0.0117 0.0061 0.3413 0.0406))
