  ("18" 0.1924 0.2106 0.1076 0.1194 0.1194 0.0117 nil nil 0.0182)
  ("CHAPTER 1. INTRODUCTION" 0.5351 0.8120 0.1044 0.1212 0.1212 0.0168 0.0145 0.2489 0.0926))
  (19
  ("Chapter 2" 0.1924 0.3646 0.2261 0.2482 0.2482 0.0221 0.0131 0.2343 0.1396)
  ("First Steps" 0.1924 0.4164 0.2833 0.3097 0.3097 0.0265 0.0155 0.2380 0.0984)
  ("In this chapter we take our ﬁrst proper steps with Haskell. We start by intro-" 0.1924 0.8117 0.3595 0.3712 0.3712 0.0117 0.0058 0.2775 0.0168)
  ("ducing the Hugs system and the standard prelude, then explain the notation" 0.1924 0.8116 0.3756 0.3873 0.3873 0.0117 0.0064 0.2377 0.0531)
  ("for function application, develop our ﬁrst Haskell script, and conclude by dis-" 0.1924 0.8121 0.3917 0.4034 0.4034 0.0117 0.0060 0.2514 0.0220)
  ("cussing a number of syntactic conventions concerning scripts." 0.1924 0.6842 0.4078 0.4195 0.4195 0.0117 0.0062 0.2260 0.0574)
  ("2.1" 0.1924 0.2271 0.4534 0.4687 0.4687 0.0153 nil 0.2860 0.0347)
  ("The Hugs system" 0.2544 0.4603 0.4534 0.4687 0.4687 0.0153 0.0091 0.2652 0.0464)
  ("As we saw in the previous chapter, small Haskell examples can be executed by" 0.1924 0.8117 0.4879 0.4996 0.4996 0.0117 0.0053 0.3078 0.0209)
  ("hand. In practice, however, we usually require an implementation of Haskell" 0.1924 0.8115 0.5038 0.5156 0.5156 0.0117 0.0068 0.2748 0.0450)
  ("that can execute programs automatically. In this book we use an interac-" 0.1924 0.8115 0.5199 0.5317 0.5317 0.0117 0.0084 0.2404 0.0336)
  ("tive system called Hugs, which is the most widely used implementation of" 0.1924 0.8118 0.5360 0.5478 0.5478 0.0117 0.0085 0.2904 0.0293)
  ("Haskell 98, the recently deﬁned stable version of the language." 0.1924 0.6927 0.5521 0.5639 0.5639 0.0117 0.0063 0.2279 0.0573)
  ("The interactive nature of Hugs makes it well suited for teaching and proto-" 0.2208 0.8118 0.5697 0.5814 0.5814 0.0117 0.0051 0.2905 0.0317)
  ("typing purposes, and its performance is suﬃcient for most applications. How-" 0.1924 0.8117 0.5856 0.5974 0.5974 0.0117 0.0057 0.3039 0.0509)
  ("ever, if greater performance or a stand-alone executable version of a Haskell" 0.1924 0.8115 0.6017 0.6135 0.6135 0.0117 0.0071 0.2223 0.0376)
  ("program is required, a number of compilers for Haskell 98 are also available," 0.1924 0.8114 0.6178 0.6296 0.6296 0.0117 0.0068 0.2772 0.0674)
  ("of which the most widely used is the Glasgow Haskell Compiler." 0.1924 0.7072 0.6340 0.6457 0.6457 0.0117 0.0063 0.3284 0.0147)
  ("2.2" 0.1924 0.2271 0.6797 0.6950 0.6950 0.0153 nil 0.2906 0.0346)
  ("The standard prelude" 0.2544 0.5112 0.6797 0.6950 0.6950 0.0153 0.0091 0.2170 0.0464)
  ("When the Hugs system is started it ﬁrst loads a library ﬁle called Prelude.hs," 0.1924 0.8118 0.7140 0.7258 0.7258 0.0117 0.0059 0.3064 0.0474)
  ("and then displays a > prompt to indicate that the system is waiting for the" 0.1924 0.8119 0.7301 0.7419 0.7419 0.0117 0.0069 0.2624 0.0296)
  ("user to enter an expression to be evaluated. For example, the library ﬁle" 0.1924 0.8120 0.7463 0.7580 0.7580 0.0117 0.0088 0.2319 0.0328)
  ("deﬁnes many familiar functions that operate on integers, including the ﬁve" 0.1924 0.8119 0.7624 0.7741 0.7741 0.0117 0.0081 0.2421 0.0542)
  ("main arithmetic operations of addition, subtraction, multiplication, division," 0.1924 0.8119 0.7783 0.7900 0.7900 0.0117 0.0070 0.2969 0.0396)
  ("and exponentiation, as illustrated below:" 0.1924 0.5192 0.7944 0.8062 0.8062 0.0117 0.0064 0.2539 0.0296)
  ("> 2+3" 0.2386 0.3038 0.8329 0.8446 0.8446 0.0117 0.0101 0.2101 0.0143)
  ("5" 0.2384 0.2475 0.8489 0.8606 0.8606 0.0117 nil nil 0.0092)
  ("19" 0.4929 0.5111 0.8975 0.9092 0.9092 0.0117 nil nil 0.0182))
  (20
  ("CHAPTER 2. FIRST STEPS" 0.5645 0.8121 0.1044 0.1212 0.1212 0.0168 0.0061 0.2544 0.0926)
  ("20" 0.1924 0.2106 0.1076 0.1194 0.1194 0.0117 nil nil 0.0182)
  ("> 2−3" 0.2384 0.3034 0.1409 0.1577 0.1558 0.0168 0.0101 0.2109 0.0143)
  ("−1" 0.2384 0.2617 0.1570 0.1738 0.1738 0.0168 nil nil 0.0233)
  ("> 2∗3" 0.2384 0.2984 0.1893 0.2061 0.2043 0.0168 0.0101 0.1216 0.0143)
  ("6" 0.2384 0.2475 0.2087 0.2204 0.2204 0.0117 nil nil 0.0092)
  ("> 7 ‘div ‘ 2" 0.2384 0.3248 0.2410 0.2528 0.2528 0.0117 0.0042 0.4812 0.0143)
  ("3" 0.2384 0.2475 0.2571 0.2689 0.2689 0.0117 nil nil 0.0092)
  ("> 2↑3" 0.2384 0.2984 0.2862 0.3031 0.3012 0.0168 0.0101 0.1216 0.0143)
  ("8" 0.2384 0.2475 0.3056 0.3173 0.3173 0.0117 nil nil 0.0092)
  ("Note that the integer division operator is written as ‘div‘, and rounds down" 0.1924 0.8119 0.3369 0.3487 0.3487 0.0117 0.0071 0.2369 0.0380)
  ("to the nearest integer if the result is a proper fraction." 0.1924 0.6290 0.3530 0.3648 0.3648 0.0117 0.0062 0.2078 0.0162)
  ("Following normal mathematical convention, exponentiation has higher pri-" 0.2208 0.8121 0.3693 0.3810 0.3810 0.0117 0.0053 0.3032 0.0757)
  ("ority than multiplication and division, which in turn have higher priority than" 0.1924 0.8119 0.3854 0.3971 0.3971 0.0117 0.0053 0.2930 0.0375)
  ("addition and subtraction. For example, 2 ∗ 3 ↑ 4 means 2 ∗ (3 ↑ 4), while 2 + 3 ∗ 4" 0.1924 0.8119 0.3982 0.4151 0.4132 0.0117 0.0023 0.4156 0.0661)
  ("means 2 + (3 ∗ 4). Moreover, exponentiation associates (brackets) to the right," 0.1924 0.8116 0.4143 0.4312 0.4293 0.0117 0.0056 0.3033 0.0500)
  ("while the other four arithmetic operators associate to the left. For example," 0.1924 0.8117 0.4337 0.4454 0.4454 0.0117 0.0070 0.2522 0.0418)
  ("2 ↑ 3 ↑ 4 means 2 ↑ (3 ↑ 4), while 2 − 3 + 4 means (2 − 3) + 4. In practice, however," 0.1924 0.8116 0.4464 0.4633 0.4614 0.0117 0.0021 0.4784 0.0092)
  ("it is often clearer to use explicit parentheses in such arithmetic expressions," 0.1924 0.8118 0.4658 0.4775 0.4775 0.0117 0.0075 0.2202 0.0122)
  ("rather than relying on the above conventions." 0.1924 0.5576 0.4819 0.4936 0.4936 0.0117 0.0063 0.1974 0.0489)
  ("In addition to functions on integers, the library ﬁle also provides a range" 0.2208 0.8120 0.4983 0.5100 0.5100 0.0117 0.0068 0.2176 0.0169)
  ("of useful functions that operate on lists. In Haskell, the elements of a list are" 0.1924 0.8118 0.5142 0.5260 0.5260 0.0117 0.0063 0.2610 0.0147)
  ("enclosed in square parentheses, and are separated by commas. Some of the" 0.1924 0.8119 0.5303 0.5421 0.5421 0.0117 0.0075 0.2331 0.0660)
  ("most commonly used library functions on lists are illustrated below." 0.1924 0.7387 0.5464 0.5582 0.5582 0.0117 0.0062 0.3015 0.0387)
  ("• Select the ﬁrst element of a non-empty list:" 0.2200 0.5857 0.5745 0.5914 0.5895 0.0117 0.0062 0.2812 0.0092)
  ("> head [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]" 0.2789 0.4369 0.6092 0.6209 0.6209 0.0117 0.0030 0.4106 0.0143)
  ("1" 0.2787 0.2879 0.6253 0.6370 0.6370 0.0117 nil nil 0.0092)
  ("• Remove the ﬁrst element from a non-empty list:" 0.2200 0.6242 0.6533 0.6702 0.6683 0.0117 0.0063 0.3170 0.0092)
  ("> tail [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]" 0.2789 0.4272 0.6881 0.6998 0.6998 0.0117 0.0030 0.4364 0.0143)
  ("[2, 3, 4, 5]" 0.2787 0.3526 0.7041 0.7158 0.7158 0.0117 0.0030 0.4315 0.0208)
  ("• Select the nth element of list (counting from zero):" 0.2200 0.6472 0.7322 0.7490 0.7471 0.0117 0.0062 0.2729 0.0092)
  ("> [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] !! 2" 0.2789 0.4226 0.7669 0.7786 0.7786 0.0117 0.0030 0.4689 0.0143)
  ("3" 0.2787 0.2879 0.7830 0.7947 0.7947 0.0117 nil nil 0.0092)
  ("• Select the ﬁrst n elements of a list:" 0.2200 0.5191 0.8110 0.8278 0.8259 0.0117 0.0062 0.2558 0.0092)
  ("> take 3 [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]" 0.2789 0.4474 0.8457 0.8575 0.8574 0.0117 0.0030 0.4071 0.0143)
