  (22
  ("CHAPTER 2. FIRST STEPS" 0.5645 0.8121 0.1044 0.1212 0.1212 0.0168 0.0061 0.2544 0.0926)
  ("22" 0.1924 0.2106 0.1076 0.1194 0.1194 0.0117 nil nil 0.0182)
  ("2.3" 0.1924 0.2270 0.1413 0.1566 0.1566 0.0153 nil 0.2896 0.0346)
  ("Function application" 0.2544 0.4971 0.1413 0.1566 0.1566 0.0153 0.0089 0.2411 0.1038)
  ("In mathematics, the application of a function to its arguments is usually de-" 0.1924 0.8120 0.1730 0.1848 0.1848 0.0117 0.0067 0.2767 0.0168)
  ("noted by enclosing the arguments in parentheses, while the multiplication of" 0.1924 0.8118 0.1892 0.2009 0.2009 0.0117 0.0069 0.2589 0.0447)
  ("two values is often denoted silently, by writing the two values next to one" 0.1924 0.8119 0.2053 0.2170 0.2170 0.0117 0.0084 0.2193 0.0283)
  ("another. For example, in mathematics the expression" 0.1924 0.6199 0.2214 0.2331 0.2331 0.0117 0.0064 0.2860 0.0662)
  ("f (a, b) + c d" 0.4552 0.5490 0.2500 0.2617 0.2617 0.0117 0.0030 0.5038 0.0090)
  ("means apply the function f to two arguments a and b, and add the result" 0.1924 0.8119 0.2788 0.2905 0.2905 0.0117 0.0081 0.2151 0.0500)
  ("to the product of c and d. Reﬂecting its primary status in the language," 0.1924 0.8115 0.2949 0.3066 0.3066 0.0117 0.0088 0.2325 0.0162)
  ("function application in Haskell is denoted silently using spacing, while the" 0.1924 0.8120 0.3110 0.3227 0.3227 0.0117 0.0087 0.2526 0.0657)
  ("multiplication of two values is denoted explicitly using the operator ∗. For" 0.1924 0.8115 0.3238 0.3407 0.3388 0.0117 0.0079 0.2506 0.1111)
  ("example, the expression above would be written in Haskell as follows:" 0.1924 0.7505 0.3431 0.3548 0.3548 0.0117 0.0062 0.2866 0.0707)
  ("f a b +c∗d" 0.4477 0.5462 0.3707 0.3876 0.3857 0.0117 0.0066 0.4108 0.0056)
  ("Moreover, function application has higher priority than all other operators." 0.1924 0.8120 0.4005 0.4122 0.4122 0.0117 0.0079 0.2343 0.0791)
  ("For example, f a + b means (f a) + b. The following table gives a few fur-" 0.1924 0.8121 0.4166 0.4283 0.4283 0.0117 0.0074 0.3072 0.0266)
  ("ther examples to illustrate the diﬀerences between the notation for function" 0.1924 0.8116 0.4327 0.4444 0.4444 0.0117 0.0074 0.2277 0.0326)
  ("application in mathematics and in Haskell:" 0.1924 0.5362 0.4488 0.4605 0.4605 0.0117 0.0062 0.3141 0.0883)
  ("Mathematics" 0.4052 0.5082 0.4780 0.4898 0.4898 0.0117 nil 0.3664 0.1030)
  ("f (x)" 0.4052 0.4409 0.4970 0.5087 0.5087 0.0117 0.0021 0.4412 0.0090)
  ("f (x, y)" 0.4052 0.4587 0.5131 0.5248 0.5248 0.0117 0.0030 0.4636 0.0090)
  ("f (g(x))" 0.4052 0.4643 0.5292 0.5409 0.5409 0.0117 0.0021 0.3369 0.0090)
  ("f (x, g(y))" 0.4052 0.4821 0.5453 0.5570 0.5570 0.0117 0.0030 0.3851 0.0090)
