  ("Note that parentheses are still required in the Haskell expression f (g x )" 0.1924 0.8119 0.5915 0.6032 0.6032 0.0117 0.0092 0.2447 0.0380)
  ("above, because f g x on its own would be interpreted as the application of" 0.1924 0.8118 0.6074 0.6192 0.6192 0.0117 0.0078 0.2098 0.0507)
  ("the function f to two arguments g and x , whereas the intention is that f is" 0.1924 0.8117 0.6235 0.6353 0.6353 0.0117 0.0073 0.2533 0.0255)
  ("applied to one argument, namely the result of applying the function g to an" 0.1924 0.8117 0.6397 0.6514 0.6514 0.0117 0.0068 0.2490 0.0580)
  ("argument x . A similar remark holds for the expression f x (g y)." 0.1924 0.7126 0.6558 0.6675 0.6675 0.0117 0.0062 0.3047 0.0759)
  ("2.4" 0.1924 0.2270 0.6937 0.7090 0.7090 0.0153 nil 0.2863 0.0346)
  ("Haskell scripts" 0.2544 0.4270 0.6937 0.7090 0.7090 0.0153 0.0092 0.3006 0.0859)
  ("As well as the functions provided in the standard prelude, it is also possible to" 0.1924 0.8118 0.7254 0.7372 0.7372 0.0117 0.0053 0.2829 0.0209)
  ("deﬁne new functions. New functions cannot be deﬁned at the > prompt within" 0.1924 0.8119 0.7416 0.7533 0.7533 0.0117 0.0046 0.3164 0.0471)
  ("Hugs, but must be deﬁned within a script, a text ﬁle comprising a sequence" 0.1924 0.8118 0.7577 0.7694 0.7694 0.0117 0.0068 0.2587 0.0454)
  ("of deﬁnitions. By convention, Haskell scripts usually have a .hs suﬃx on their" 0.1924 0.8117 0.7738 0.7855 0.7855 0.0117 0.0057 0.2782 0.0147)
  ("ﬁlename to diﬀerentiate them from other kinds of ﬁles." 0.1924 0.6323 0.7899 0.8016 0.8016 0.0117 0.0062 0.2939 0.0663)
  ("My first script" 0.1924 0.3361 0.8236 0.8364 0.8364 0.0128 0.0076 0.3896 0.0333)
  ("When developing a Haskell script, it is useful to keep two windows open, one" 0.1924 0.8119 0.8489 0.8606 0.8606 0.0117 0.0064 0.3047 0.0473)
